her with its garden and symmetrical complex; it is a funerary monument, not a royal residence.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1632–1653","era":"c. 1632–1653","period103":"baroque","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Mughal"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-02","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-02"],"start106":1632,"end106":1653,"placeId":"WORLD106-02","photos":[],"aliases":["Taj Mahal"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-003","ARCHED-WORLD106-004"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-02"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-03","title":"Borobudur","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/","country":"Indonesia","city":"Central Java","lat":-7.608,"lon":110.204,"style":"Buddhist; Syailendra","note":"A Buddhist monument with square terraces below circular platforms and a crowning stupa. The ascending route organizes reliefs and repeated stupas into a spatial sequence.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 750–850","era":"c. 750–850","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Buddhist; Syailendra"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-03","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-03"],"start106":750,"end106":850,"placeId":"WORLD106-03","photos":[],"aliases":["Borobudur"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-005","ARCHED-WORLD106-006"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-03"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-04","title":"Petra","kind":"archaeology","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/","country":"Jordan","city":"Wadi Musa","lat":30.329,"lon":35.444,"style":"Nabataean; Hellenistic influences","note":"Nabataean Petra combined caravan commerce, rock-cut architecture and careful water management. Carved facades do not mean that every part of the city was cut into rock.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 300 BCE–100","era":"c. 300 BCE–100","period103":"ancient","kinds103":["archaeology"],"styles103":["Nabataean; Hellenistic influences"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-04","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-04"],"start106":-300,"end106":100,"placeId":"WORLD106-04","photos":[],"aliases":["Petra"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-007","ARCHED-WORLD106-008"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-04"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-05","title":"Machu Picchu","kind":"archaeology","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/","country":"Peru","city":"Cusco region","lat":-13.163,"lon":-72.545,"style":"Inca","note":"The Inca site joins stone buildings, terraces and steep mountain terrain. Its fifteenth-century setting distinguishes it from earlier Andean cultures such as the Moche.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1400–1500","era":"c. 1400–1500","period103":"renaissance","kinds103":["archaeology"],"styles103":["Inca"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-05","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-05"],"start106":1400,"end106":1500,"placeId":"WORLD106-05","photos":[],"aliases":["Machu Picchu"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-009","ARCHED-WORLD106-010"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-05"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-06","title":"Chichén Itzá","kind":"archaeology","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/483/","country":"Mexico","city":"Yucatán","lat":20.684,"lon":-88.568,"style":"Maya; Toltec connections","note":"Temple pyramids, a large ball court and other monuments show a Maya city with wider Mesoamerican connections. Do not confuse it with Teotihuacan in central Mexico.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/483/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 600–1200","era":"c. 600–1200","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["archaeology"],"styles103":["Maya; Toltec connections"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-06","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-06"],"start106":600,"end106":1200,"placeId":"WORLD106-06","photos":[],"aliases":["Chichen Itza"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-011","ARCHED-WORLD106-012"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-06"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-07","title":"Teotihuacan","kind":"archaeology","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/414/","country":"Mexico","city":"Valley of Mexico","lat":19.692,"lon":-98.844,"style":"Mesoamerican urbanism","note":"The Avenue of the Dead links monumental precincts including the Sun and Moon pyramids. The city flourished many centuries before the Aztec Empire; its builders should not be labelled Aztec.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/414/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1–700","era":"c. 1–700","period103":"ancient","kinds103":["archaeology"],"styles103":["Mesoamerican urbanism"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-07","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-07"],"start106":1,"end106":700,"placeId":"WORLD106-07","photos":[],"aliases":["Teotihuacan"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-013","ARCHED-WORLD106-014"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-07"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-08","title":"Rapa Nui: moai","kind":"sculpture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/715/","country":"Chile","city":"Rapa Nui","lat":-27.125,"lon":-109.277,"style":"Polynesian; Rapa Nui","note":"The moai are monumental stone figures associated with a Polynesian island society. Many stand on ceremonial platforms called ahu. The range is a broad period, not a date for every statue.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/715/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1000–1600","era":"c. 1000–1600","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["sculpture"],"styles103":["Polynesian; Rapa Nui"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-08","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-08"],"start106":1000,"end106":1600,"placeId":"WORLD106-08","photos":[],"aliases":["Rapa Nui National Park"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-015","ARCHED-WORLD106-016"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-08"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-09","title":"Himeji Castle","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/","country":"Japan","city":"Himeji","lat":34.839,"lon":134.694,"style":"Japanese castle architecture","note":"White plastered surfaces cover a sophisticated timber castle. Gates, walls and routes form a defensive system: its appearance is not simply ornamental.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1601–1609","era":"c. 1601–1609","period103":"baroque","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Japanese castle architecture"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-09","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-09"],"start106":1601,"end106":1609,"placeId":"WORLD106-09","photos":[],"aliases":["Himeji Castle"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-017","ARCHED-WORLD106-018"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-09"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-10","title":"Forbidden City","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/439/","country":"China","city":"Beijing","lat":39.916,"lon":116.397,"style":"Ming and Qing imperial architecture","note":"Beijing's imperial palace developed as a hierarchy of courts and halls along an axis. The initial Ming construction dates do not imply that every surviving structure is original.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/439/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1406–1420","era":"c. 1406–1420","period103":"renaissance","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Ming and Qing imperial architecture"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-10","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-10"],"start106":1406,"end106":1420,"placeId":"WORLD106-10","photos":[],"aliases":["Forbidden City"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-019","ARCHED-WORLD106-020"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-10"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-11","title":"Temple of Heaven","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/881/","country":"China","city":"Beijing","lat":39.883,"lon":116.407,"style":"Chinese imperial ritual architecture","note":"This ceremonial complex relates imperial ritual to ideas of heaven and earth. Read its symbolism and layout as a religious-political landscape rather than a residential palace.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/881/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1406–1420","era":"c. 1406–1420","period103":"renaissance","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Chinese imperial ritual architecture"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-11","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-11"],"start106":1406,"end106":1420,"placeId":"WORLD106-11","photos":[],"aliases":["Temple of Heaven"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-021","ARCHED-WORLD106-022"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-11"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-12","title":"Potala Palace","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/707/","country":"China","city":"Lhasa","lat":29.658,"lon":91.117,"style":"Tibetan Buddhist architecture","note":"The palace was a winter residence of the Dalai Lama and a centre of Tibetan Buddhist authority. Its major seventeenth-century development stands on a site with earlier history.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/707/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1600–1700","era":"c. 1600–1700","period103":"baroque","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Tibetan Buddhist architecture"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-12","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-12"],"start106":1600,"end106":1700,"placeId":"WORLD106-12","photos":[],"aliases":["Potala Palace"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-023","ARCHED-WORLD106-024"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-12"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-13","title":"Lalibela: rock-hewn churches","kind":"church","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/","country":"Ethiopia","city":"Lalibela","lat":12.032,"lon":39.043,"style":"Ethiopian Christian rock-hewn architecture","note":"Eleven medieval churches were carved from rock and connected by trenches and passages. Excavating spaces and leaving structural masses produces architecture by subtraction.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1200–1300","era":"c. 1200–1300","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["church"],"styles103":["Ethiopian Christian rock-hewn architecture"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-13","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-13"],"start106":1200,"end106":1300,"placeId":"WORLD106-13","photos":[],"aliases":["Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-025","ARCHED-WORLD106-026"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-13"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-14","title":"Great Zimbabwe","kind":"archaeology","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","country":"Zimbabwe","city":"Masvingo","lat":-20.267,"lon":30.933,"style":"Shona stone architecture","note":"The ruins record a Shona urban and trading centre. Carefully fitted granite walls, especially the Great Enclosure, challenge the idea that monumental African architecture was limited to Egypt.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1100–1500","era":"c. 1100–1500","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["archaeology"],"styles103":["Shona stone architecture"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-14","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-14"],"start106":1100,"end106":1500,"placeId":"WORLD106-14","photos":[],"aliases":["Great Zimbabwe"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-027","ARCHED-WORLD106-028"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-14"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-15","title":"Djenné: historic town","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","country":"Mali","city":"Djenné","lat":13.906,"lon":-4.555,"style":"Sahelian earthen architecture","note":"Earthen construction links the town's buildings to local materials and continuing maintenance. The range describes a historic urban phase, not the construction date of today's Great Mosque.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1400–1600","era":"c. 1400–1600","period103":"renaissance","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Sahelian earthen architecture"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-15","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-15"],"start106":1400,"end106":1600,"placeId":"WORLD106-15","photos":[],"aliases":["Old Towns of Djenné"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-029","ARCHED-WORLD106-030"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-15"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-16","title":"Persepolis","kind":"archaeology","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/114/","country":"Iran","city":"Fars","lat":29.935,"lon":52.891,"style":"Achaemenid Persian","note":"Darius I began the Achaemenid palace complex on a monumental terrace. Reliefs and ceremonial spaces expressed imperial authority; this is not a Greek polis.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/114/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 518 BCE–330 BCE","era":"c. 518 BCE–330 BCE","period103":"ancient","kinds103":["archaeology"],"styles103":["Achaemenid Persian"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-16","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-16"],"start106":-518,"end106":-330,"placeId":"WORLD106-16","photos":[],"aliases":["Persepolis"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-031","ARCHED-WORLD106-032"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-16"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-17","title":"Sanchi","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/524/","country":"India","city":"Madhya Pradesh","lat":23.479,"lon":77.739,"style":"Buddhist stupas","note":"Stupas, gateways and other remains trace a long Buddhist history beginning in the Mauryan period. The stupa is a sacred mound or reliquary monument, not an ordinary dwelling.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/524/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 300 BCE–1200","era":"c. 300 BCE–1200","period103":"ancient","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Buddhist stupas"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-17","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-17"],"start106":-300,"end106":1200,"placeId":"WORLD106-17","photos":[],"aliases":["Buddhist Monuments at Sanchi"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-033","ARCHED-WORLD106-034"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-17"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-18","title":"Khajuraho","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/240/","country":"India","city":"Madhya Pradesh","lat":24.852,"lon":79.92,"style":"Chandella; Hindu and Jain temples","note":"The surviving temples combine towered sanctuaries with extensive sculpture. Both Hindu and Jain monuments are represented; reducing the site to erotic imagery misses its wider religious programme.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/240/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 950–1050","era":"c. 950–1050","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Chandella; Hindu and Jain temples"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-18","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-18"],"start106":950,"end106":1050,"placeId":"WORLD106-18","photos":[],"aliases":["Khajuraho Group of Monuments"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-035","ARCHED-WORLD106-036"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-18"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-19","title":"Bagan","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1588/","country":"Myanmar","city":"Mandalay region","lat":21.173,"lon":94.859,"style":"Buddhist sacred landscape","note":"Thousands of Buddhist monuments form a landscape shaped by patronage and merit-making. The selected range describes the principal building era, not every restoration.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1588/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1000–1300","era":"c. 1000–1300","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Buddhist sacred landscape"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-19","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-19"],"start106":1000,"end106":1300,"placeId":"WORLD106-19","photos":[],"aliases":["Bagan"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-037","ARCHED-WORLD106-038"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-19"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-20","title":"Samarkand","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/603/","country":"Uzbekistan","city":"Samarkand","lat":39.655,"lon":66.975,"style":"Timurid","note":"Samarkand links Silk Road exchange with Timurid monuments and scholarship. The Registan, Bibi-Khanum Mosque and Ulugh Beg's observatory have different functions and building histories.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/603/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1300–1500","era":"c. 1300–1500","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Timurid"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-20","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-20"],"start106":1300,"end106":1500,"placeId":"WORLD106-20","photos":[],"aliases":["Samarkand – Crossroad of Cultures"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-039","ARCHED-WORLD106-040"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-20"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-21","title":"Hampi","kind":"archaeology","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/241/","country":"India","city":"Karnataka","lat":15.335,"lon":76.462,"style":"Vijayanagara; Dravidian","note":"The former Vijayanagara capital brings together temples, royal structures and a dramatic rocky landscape. The ruins belong to a major southern Indian kingdom, not to Mughal Agra.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/241/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1300–1600","era":"c. 1300–1600","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["archaeology"],"styles103":["Vijayanagara; Dravidian"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-21","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-21"],"start106":1300,"end106":1600,"placeId":"WORLD106-21","photos":[],"aliases":["Group of Monuments at Hampi"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-041","ARCHED-WORLD106-042"],"tapestryIds":["t106-WORLD106-21"],"museumIds":[]},{"id":"LE106-WORLD106-22","title":"Konark Sun Temple","kind":"architecture","reference":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/246/","country":"India","city":"Odisha","lat":19.887,"lon":86.094,"style":"Kalinga; Hindu","note":"The temple gives architectural form to the chariot of Surya, the sun god. Carved wheels and horses turn an entire building into a religious image.","coordinateSource":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/246/maps/","coordinatePrecision106":"Representative site position, rounded; not a boundary or entrance","built":"c. 1200–1300","era":"c. 1200–1300","period103":"medieval","kinds103":["architecture"],"styles103":["Kalinga; Hindu"],"entityId":"LE106-WORLD106-22","photo":null,"mapIds":["WORLD106-22"],"start106":1200,"end106":1300,"placeId":"WORLD106-22","photos":[],"aliases":["Sun Temple, Konârak"],"questionIds":["ARCHED-WORLD106-